//? Title: Lamport clocks through actual message events
//? Subtitle: Original two-process exchange, concurrency limits and checked timestamp boundaries
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Distributed processes need to respect causal event order without relying on a
shared physical clock. Lamport clocks assign scalar logical timestamps by
incrementing locally and advancing past received stamps. We inspect actual
pre/post transitions, retain the original seven-event exchange, and show why
increasing scalar time does not prove causality. Finite signed64 limits are
checked before mutation rather than permitting undefined overflow.
)

\toc

> The clock condition and its one-way implication

Let happened-before be generated by program order within each process, a send
before its matching receive, and transitivity. Each local/send event increments
its clock. A receive stamped M commits

$$ C_{new}=\max(C_{old},M)+1. $$
\alttext(The receiver advances to one greater than the larger of its local clock and the message timestamp.)

An event’s local successor has a larger timestamp, and a receive has a larger
timestamp than its actual send stamp. Induction along a happened-before path
gives a→b implies C(a)<C(b). The converse is false: independent processes can
accumulate different local counts without communicating. Equal timestamps also
occur. A consistent process-id tie-break can extend this partial order to a
total order, but the scalar clock alone does not identify causality or physical
elapsed time.

The native class owns one process’s counter only. Callers must attach actual
send stamps and record every relevant local event; the API does not authenticate
messages, infer network edges, synchronize threads or detect omitted events.
Actual transition records retain before, original receive stamp and committed
after, using the same kernel as historical scalar methods.

> One receive with explicit inputs

A new process receiving the synthetic nonnegative stamp5 advances from0 to6.
This is a checked numerical control, not a claim that a particular network send
occurred.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/lamport_receive.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/lamport_clock.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    dm::algorithms::LamportClock c;
    const auto e = c.receive_step(5);
    std::print("| actual local before | actual received stamp | actual committed after |\n| ---: | ---: | " "---: |\n| {} | {} | {} |\n",
               e.before, e.message_time, e.after);
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    d.node("local", {0, 1}, std::format("Actual local before\n{}", e.before)).node("message", {0, -1}, std::format("Actual received stamp\n{}", e.message_time)).node("after", {7, 0}, std::format("Actual max plus1\n{}", e.after)).edge("local", "after").edge("message", "after").save_svg("build/lamport_receive.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| actual local before | actual received stamp | actual committed after |
| ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 5 | 6 |
\image(figures/lamport_receive.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual max-plus-one receive inputs and committed output.)
\alttext(The synthetic receive combines actual local0 with received5 and commits6; no network provenance is inferred.)

> Original two-process exchange

Keep the original seven events: P0 local, P0 send m1, P1 local, P1 receive m1,
P1 send m2, P0 receive m2, P0 local. Message stamps are taken directly from
actual send records. The plot shows both clock snapshots after each scheduled
event; a carried unchanged value for the other process is context, not an event
on that process. The horizontal axis is illustrative schedule ordinal, not
physical seconds.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/lamport_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/lamport_clock.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    dm::algorithms::LamportClock p0, p1;
    std::vector<double> x, a, b;
    std::print("| original schedule ordinal | original event | actual before | incoming stamp or0 | actual " "after | P0 snapshot | P1 snapshot |\n| ---: | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    auto show = [&](const char *name, const dm::algorithms::LamportEvent & e) {
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", x.size(), name,
                   e.before, e.message_time, e.after, p0.time(), p1.time());
        x.push_back(x.size());
        a.push_back(p0.time());
        b.push_back(p1.time());
    };
    show("P0 local", p0.local_step());
    const auto m1 = p0.send_step();
    show("P0 send m1", m1);
    show("P1 local", p1.local_step());
    const auto r1 = p1.receive_step(m1.after);
    show("P1 receive m1", r1);
    const auto m2 = p1.send_step();
    show("P1 send m2", m2);
    show("P0 receive m2", p0.receive_step(m2.after));
    show("P0 local", p0.local_step());
    std::print("\nActual m1 send {}; actual m1 receive {}; strict send-before-receive {}.\n",
               m1.after, r1.after, m1.after < r1.after);
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.line(x, a, "P0", {37, 99, 235}, 2).points(x, a, "", {37, 99, 235}, 4).line(x, b, "P1", {220, 38, 38}, 2).points(x, b, "", {220, 38, 38}, 4).y_limits(-.5, 8).title("Original schedule: actual two-process clock snapshots").x_label("original scheduled event ordinal").y_label("actual logical clock").save_svg("build/lamport_original.svg");
}
```
