> Scalar order does not imply causality

Now perform one local event on A and two on B, with no messages. A’s event has
time1 and B’s second has time2, but there is no happened-before path between
them. The diagram includes only the actual within-B program-order edge; no
cross-process arrow is invented from timestamp order.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/lamport_concurrent.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/lamport_clock.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    dm::algorithms::LamportClock a, b;
    const auto a1 = a.local_step(), b1 = b.local_step(), b2 = b.local_step();
    std::print("| actual process/event | actual timestamp |\n| --- | ---: |\n| A first local | {} |\n| B " "first local | {} |\n| B second local | {} |\n",
               a1.after, b1.after, b2.after);
    std::print("\nA first time less than B second time {}; messages in this complete control 0.\n",
               a1.after < b2.after);
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    d.node("a", {0, 3}, std::format("Actual A local\nC={}", a1.after)).node(
        "b1", {0, 0}, std::format("Actual B first local\nC={}", b1.after)).node(
        "b2", {7, 0}, std::format("Actual B second local\nC={}", b2.after)).edge("b1", "b2").save_svg("build/lamport_concurrent.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| actual process/event | actual timestamp |
| --- | ---: |
| A first local | 1 |
| B first local | 1 |
| B second local | 2 |

A first time less than B second time true; messages in this complete control 0.
\image(figures/lamport_concurrent.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Independent processes can have strictly ordered scalar timestamps without causality.)
\alttext(Only B’s actual local program-order edge is drawn; A’s lower scalar time does not imply a cross-process dependency.)

> Complete chains and the finite clock boundary

Alternate sends between two processes, using every actual receive as the
predecessor of the next send. A chain of n messages contains2n causally ordered
send/receive events, so from zero clocks its largest stamp is2n. This checks a
complete family of native message chains, not a physical-delay or
network-throughput benchmark.

A receive with an old stamp still advances past the current clock. Incoming
negatives are invalid. If max(current,incoming) is INT64_MAX, the next increment
cannot be represented: validation throws before mutation. Receiving INT64_MAX−1
is permitted and reaches the last representable value; a subsequent send fails
atomically.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/lamport_chains.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/lamport_clock.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <array>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<double> x, y;
    std::print("| actual messages | actual send and receive events | final maximum logical stamp | exact " "complete-chain value |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (int n : {1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32}) {
        std::array<dm::algorithms::LamportClock, 2> c;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
            const auto s = c[i % 2].send_step();
            c[1 - i % 2].receive_step(s.after);
        }
        const auto final = std::max(c[0].time(), c[1].time());
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", n, 2 * n, final, 2 * n);
        x.push_back(n);
        y.push_back(final);
    }
    dm::algorithms::LamportClock old;
    old.receive(100);
    const auto stale = old.receive_step(2);
    dm::algorithms::LamportClock edge;
    edge.receive(std::numeric_limits<std::int64_t>::max() - 1);
    const auto before = edge.time();
    bool overflow = false;
    try {
        edge.send_step();
    } catch (const std::overflow_error &) {
        overflow = true;
    }
    std::print("\n| old-stamp receive before | actual incoming old stamp | actual after | maximum send " "rejected | boundary state unchanged |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | --- | --- |\n| {} | {} | {} | " "{} | {} |\n",
               stale.before, stale.message_time, stale.after, overflow,
               edge.time() == before);
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.line(x, y, "actual", {37, 99, 235}, 2).points(x, y, "", {37, 99, 235}, 4).title("Complete causal chains: actual two ticks per message").x_label("actual message count").y_label("maximum actual logical stamp").save_svg("build/lamport_chains.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| actual messages | actual send and receive events | final maximum logical stamp | exact complete-chain value |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 2 | 2 | 2 |
| 2 | 4 | 4 | 4 |
| 4 | 8 | 8 | 8 |
| 8 | 16 | 16 | 16 |
| 16 | 32 | 32 | 32 |
| 32 | 64 | 64 | 64 |

| old-stamp receive before | actual incoming old stamp | actual after | maximum send rejected | boundary state unchanged |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | --- | --- |
| 101 | 2 | 102 | true | true |
\image(figures/lamport_chains.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual complete message chains and atomic signed64 boundary controls.)
\alttext(Computed maxima equal twice message count; a separate full-width control records rejection before timestamp overflow.)

> Scope and verification

The scalar state is nonnegative signed64, with O(1) allocation-free transitions
and owned returned records. Copying a clock copies only its value. Independent
objects share no mutable state, while concurrent mutation of one clock needs
caller synchronization. Negative/overflow failures occur before commit. This
library models only clock updates, leaving process identities, messages, causal
graph construction and protocol correctness to the caller. No
timestamp-to-duration inference is made.

An independent integer max recurrence checked10000 actual local/send/receive
transitions, and another10000 transitions matched the frozen valid-input clock
exactly. Native tests retain the original seven-event exchange, check transitive
happened-before on explicit message/program-order graphs and verify
complete-chain identities, full-width atomic failures, copied state and existing
formatting. The ten-report shared build passed141 native CTest cases,45 directly
instrumented address/undefined-behaviour sanitizer cases,90 compiled/executed
public examples,412/412 scoped file definitions plus11/11 changed
lagged-Fibonacci engine definitions, and ten representative clangd hovers. All
four complete report programs executed and all four native figures were visually
reviewed. Receipts and repeatable checks are retained under
`build/mepml-conversion/batch-184-193/`.

> References

L. Lamport(1978),
[Time, Clocks, and the Ordering of Events in a Distributed System|https://doi.org/10.1145/359545.359563],
Communications of the ACM21(7),558–565. The finite signed64 failure policy and
actual record API are native implementation contracts.
